WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.050
This is the Daily Tech Brief for Sunday, 18 October. Black Forest Labs' Flux-3 Image Klein has landed in ComfyUI, though launch workflows required re-pointing to version v0.41.3. The community is anointing MOSS Image as the new SDXL, citing its lightweight and uncensored nature. A new Strata-based engine called Xolotl runs the full 552B DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash on a single 4090.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:27.275
Later, a closer look at Flux-3 Image Klein, SUPRA-TEX, and Xolotl.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:50.875
Black Forest Labs' Flux-3 Image Klein is the ComfyUI-native image-to-image entry of the Flux-3 line and is trending on Hugging Face. The launch was shaky, with launch-day example workflows pulled and re-pointed at ComfyUI v0.41.3. A top thread on the same build argues text-to-image cannot make realistic faces, with eyes and bigger smiles morphing. Commenters are split between a broken model and a resolution-scaling issue.

00:00:51.575 --> 00:01:13.975
The community calls MOSS Image the new SDXL. It is described as lightweight, easy to train, uncensored, and having strong base capabilities for its size. This stands in contrast to Flux-3, which users on Reddit report is mocked for being thoroughly censored. Comparisons are already landing, including complex-pose A/B tests against Krea 2, early LoRA generations, and first-time ComfyUI users trying it.

00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:34.275
SUPRA-TEX dresses a given mesh with relightable materials from one reference image. According to the paper posted to Hugging Face Daily Papers, the geometry is a fixed input. The model only produces relightable texture and materials aligned to the reference. This allows the dressed mesh to be lit like a native asset in your pipeline instead of reading as a captured screen.

00:01:34.975 --> 00:01:53.325
A trending LoRA for MiniMax H3 adds 360-degree orbit camera control over image-text-to-video. This essentially creates a generated turntable shot for an asset. The same base model is being wrapped in local tooling like the Soran't studio. Prompt recipes for reference-to-video are also circulating for it on Reddit.

00:01:54.025 --> 00:02:08.600
The new Epic Games Launcher answers long-standing speed complaints. Test reports put its speed and response in line with Steam's. This matters for anyone whose Unreal workflow depends on it syncing projects and pulling assets without the usual lag, according to users on Reddit.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:29.075
A new inference engine built on Strata called Xolotl runs all 552B of DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash on a single 4090. It achieves a speed of ~27 tok/s. This is the single-card answer to cluster setups. An uncensored 1017 quant is promised in a couple of days, according to reports on r/LocalLLaMA.

00:02:29.775 --> 00:02:45.600
Segment Display brings procedural 7 and 16 segment screens to Blender. It is a Geometry Nodes add-on run entirely from the N-panel. The display is live node rules rather than an imported mesh. Segments and layout stay editable in-scene instead of being re-exported per change.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:03:08.725
In brief. Riot's particle emitters are reborn as closed-form functions of age, making frames render correctly in any order. A per-pixel ray-traced Kerr black hole runs live in WebGPU within a single 119 KB HTML file. Crossed-fins fake light shafts show where standard volumetric tricks thin out in Godot. OpenGCUA is a fully open stack for generalist computer use, expanding past GUI-only interaction.

00:03:09.425 --> 00:03:11.875
Now, a closer look at today's top stories.

00:03:12.575 --> 00:03:32.025
Flux-3 Image Klein has arrived in ComfyUI as an open weights model for text-to-image generation and image editing. According to its GitHub page, the model uses a 5.6B parameter image transformer and Qwen3-VL-4B-Instruct as the text encoder. It accepts a prompt and up to 10 reference images to produce a single output image.

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:49.100
The repository provides model files in BF16, int8 and NVFP4 formats, with int8 versions designed for 8 GB GPUs such as the RTX 3070. Users must employ a prompt upsampler to convert short requests into full scene captions, as short prompts yield generic results.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:04:12.975
The launch included reworked workflows for ComfyUI v0.41.3, after initial examples were pulled and re-pointed. Key features include bounding box placement on a 0 to 1000 grid and text rendering within images. However, a top Reddit thread argues that the model struggles with realistic faces, specifically noting morphing eyes and smiles. Commenters are divided on whether this is a broken model or a resolution-scaling issue.

00:04:12.975 --> 00:04:20.750
The model is released under the FLUX Kommunity License, which permits non-commercial use and offers a commercial-output exception for Qualifying Users.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:44.575
A new method called SUPRA-Tex dresses a given 3D mesh with relightable materials from a single reference image. The system treats the mesh geometry as a fixed input, producing only texture and materials that align with the reference. This allows the dressed mesh to be lit like a native asset in a pipeline, rather than appearing as a captured screen. The authors note that existing methods often introduce artifacts or fail to preserve fine details.

00:04:44.575 --> 00:04:51.850
SUPRA-Tex addresses this by decoupling visible-material estimation, surface alignment, and observation-guided 3D completion.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:05:14.000
The process begins with an MLLM-based image editing model that predicts lighting-separated PBR maps in the reference view. These maps are projected onto the visible surface and encoded into native observation latents. An interleaved flow transformer then holds these latents as fixed, read-only anchors. It alternates updates for unobserved regions and the full surface to generate complete material latents.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:31.375
According to the paper, this design delivers What You See Is What You Get fidelity on visible regions. The model is trained solely on public datasets. It achieves state-of-the-art performance in geometry-conditioned texturing and end-to-end asset generation, matching or exceeding leading commercial systems on in-the-wild images.

00:05:32.075 --> 00:05:55.600
A new inference engine called Xolotl, built on Strata, allows users to run the full 552B DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash model on a single RTX 4090. According to the project's GitHub page, the setup requires a Ryzen 7 5700X, 90GB of DDR4, and an NVMe drive. The engine distributes model weights across VRAM, pinned RAM, and NVMe storage, pulling in only the experts needed for each token.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:05.525
The author reports achieving 27 tok/s decode speed and 180 to 320 tok/s prefill speed with a 512K context window.

00:06:06.225 --> 00:06:23.775
The model runs with heavy quantization. While the Engrams remain untouched, preserving 200gb of the model, the dense part uses Q_8 quantization, with experiments in Q6 for a 15% decode speed increase. Expert layers use a mix of 1, 2, and 3 bit quantization based on sensitivity assessments.

00:06:23.775 --> 00:06:38.475
Users on the discussion thread note that while the model retains significant intelligence, it takes 3 to 10 times more thinking tokens to reach the same outcome as the original. This occurs because the quantized model makes more mistakes and requires additional time to correct them.

00:06:39.175 --> 00:07:01.600
The engine also supports GLM-5.3-Flash, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, and Qwen3.8-27B. The author states they are currently optimizing for their specific hardware constraints but believes the system would work with higher quantization levels if the hardware supports it. Benchmarks cited include 21/21 on HumanEval, 10/10 tool calls, and 3/3 agentic bug-fix loops, all with thinking enabled.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.275
An uncensored 1017 quant is promised to be available in a couple of days.

